# Amarula — pair a phone & send a message

## About

A minimal [Livebook](https://livebook.dev) walkthrough of
[Amarula](https://github.com/tubedude/amarula): link a WhatsApp account by
scanning a QR code, then send a text message — all from a notebook, no local
checkout required (it installs the package from Hex).

> **⚠️ Unofficial — use at your own risk.** Amarula is not affiliated with,
> endorsed by, or sponsored by WhatsApp or Meta. WhatsApp does not support
> third-party clients, and automating an account can violate
> [WhatsApp's Terms of Service](https://www.whatsapp.com/legal/terms-of-service) —
> it may ban the number you use, with no warning and no appeal. Only link an
> account you own and can afford to lose; never use this for spam or bulk
> messaging.

**Credentials get written to disk** at `amarula_data/livebook/`, next to
wherever this notebook's runtime is working from — that's what lets
re-running the pairing cell later reconnect without a new QR. It also means
the linked device stays live until you unlink it. When you're done, delete
that directory (or unlink the device from the phone: **WhatsApp → Settings →
Linked Devices**) so a stale credential isn't left sitting on disk.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:amarula, "~> 0.5"},
  {:kino, "~> 0.14"}
])

Kino.nothing()
```

Amarula's shared supervision tree isn't auto-started (a real app starts this
once, itself, before any connection) — a notebook is no different:

```elixir
case Amarula.Supervisor.start_link() do
  {:ok, _pid} -> :ok
  {:error, {:already_started, _pid}} -> :ok
end

Kino.nothing()
```

## 1. Connect

```elixir
{:ok, conn} =
  Amarula.new(%{profile: :livebook})
  |> Amarula.connect(parent: self())

Kino.nothing()
```

## 2. Pair (scan the QR)

Run this cell and scan the code with **WhatsApp → Settings → Linked Devices →
Link a device**. It rotates every ~20s while unscanned; the frame below
updates in place each time. If this notebook already paired on a previous run,
this skips straight to "connected".

```elixir
frame = Kino.Frame.new() |> Kino.render()

render_qr = fn qr_string ->
  case QRCode.create(qr_string) |> QRCode.render(:svg) do
    {:ok, svg} -> Kino.Frame.render(frame, Kino.Image.new(svg, :svg))
    {:error, reason} -> Kino.Frame.render(frame, Kino.Markdown.new("QR render failed: #{inspect(reason)}"))
  end
end

wait_for_connection = fn wait_for_connection ->
  receive do
    {:amarula, :connection_update, %{connection: :open}} ->
      Kino.Frame.render(frame, Kino.Markdown.new("✅ **Connected.**"))
      :open

    {:amarula, :connection_update, %{qr: qr}} when is_binary(qr) ->
      render_qr.(qr)
      wait_for_connection.(wait_for_connection)

    {:amarula, :pairing_success, _data} ->
      Kino.Frame.render(frame, Kino.Markdown.new("📱 Paired — completing login…"))
      wait_for_connection.(wait_for_connection)

    {:amarula, :error, reason} ->
      Kino.Frame.render(frame, Kino.Markdown.new("❌ Connection error: `#{inspect(reason)}`"))
      {:error, reason}
  after
    180_000 ->
      Kino.Frame.render(frame, Kino.Markdown.new("⏱️ Timed out waiting to connect."))
      {:error, :timeout}
  end
end

wait_for_connection.(wait_for_connection)
Kino.nothing()
```

## 3. Send a message

A bare number becomes a PN address; anything with an `@` is parsed as-is.

```elixir
to_input = Kino.Input.text("Recipient (phone number or JID)") |> Kino.render()
text_input = Kino.Input.text("Message", default: "hello from Amarula 👋") |> Kino.render()

Kino.nothing()
```

```elixir
to_raw = Kino.Input.read(to_input)
text = Kino.Input.read(text_input)

address = if String.contains?(to_raw, "@"), do: Amarula.Address.parse(to_raw), else: Amarula.Address.pn(to_raw)

case Amarula.send_text(conn, address, text) do
  {:ok, id} -> Kino.Markdown.new("✅ sent (`#{id}`)")
  {:error, reason} -> Kino.Markdown.new("❌ failed: `#{inspect(reason)}`")
end
```

## 4. (Optional) watch incoming messages live

Spawns a background listener so the notebook keeps responding while it
watches — click **Stop listening** to end it. This re-points the connection's
event sink at the listener task, so run it after you're done pairing above.

```elixir
messages_frame = Kino.Frame.new() |> Kino.render()
stop_button = Kino.Control.button("Stop listening") |> Kino.render()

Kino.nothing()
```

```elixir
listen = fn listen ->
  receive do
    :stop ->
      Kino.Frame.append(messages_frame, Kino.Markdown.new("🛑 Stopped."))

    {:amarula, :messages_upsert, %{from: from, messages: messages}} ->
      Enum.each(messages, fn msg ->
        Kino.Frame.append(
          messages_frame,
          Kino.Markdown.new("**#{inspect(from)}** — `#{msg.type}`: #{inspect(msg.content)}")
        )
      end)

      listen.(listen)
  end
end

listener =
  Task.async(fn ->
    Amarula.set_parent(conn, self())
    Kino.Frame.append(messages_frame, Kino.Markdown.new("👂 Listening…"))
    listen.(listen)
  end)

Kino.listen(stop_button, fn _event -> send(listener.pid, :stop) end)

Kino.nothing()
```

## 5. Clean up

When you're done, unlink the device and wipe its stored credentials
(`amarula_data/livebook/`) rather than leaving them on disk:

```elixir
case Amarula.wipe_credentials(conn) do
  :ok -> Kino.Markdown.new("🧹 Credentials wiped — device unlinked.")
  {:error, reason} -> Kino.Markdown.new("❌ failed: `#{inspect(reason)}`")
end
```
